1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 { The Italian game is one of the oldest openings in chess. It was developed in the 16th and 17th centuries by famous Italian masters Damiano, Polerio, and Greco. It has always been frequently seen in beginners' and amateurs' games but its popularity among strong players gradually ceased in favor of the Ruy Lopez, which was considered a better try for an advantage until very recently. However, things have changed dramatically in recent years. Due to the futility of their attempts against the Berlin Defence, top players have started seeking ways to pose Black problems in the long-forgotten Italian. As a result of the trend set by Carlsen, Anand, Caruana, Karjakin, Vachier- Lagrave, Giri and other strong players it has suddenly become more popular than the Ruy Lopez and its theory has grown enormously. Unlike just a few years ago, Black is nowadays required to know the nuances of different lines and move orders to obtain a good position out of the opening. } Bc5 4. O-O Nf6 5. d3 O-O 6. c3 d5 { The most active approach, reminiscent to the variation I recommend against 4.d3 anti-Berlin main line. } 7. exd5 Nxd5 { We have reached one of the most important structures in this repertoire. White mainly plays against the e5-pawn and sometimes grabs space on the queenside. Black, on the other hand seeks counterplay against the d3-pawn and on the kingside, where he has the pawn majority. Typical continuations include ...Nb6, ...Bg4, ...Bd6 or ...f6 (after ...Nb6). } 8. b4 { -KEY- } Be7 { Note that } ( 8... Bb6 9. Bxd5 Qxd5 10. c4 { loses a piece. } ) 
9. Re1 { The idea of following 8. b4 with typical slow play is questionable, because White is left with a weak c3 pawn and cannot easily develop the b1 knight. } a6 10. Nxe5 { This wins a pawn but White is behind in development. } Nxe5 11. Rxe5 Be6 12. Bxd5 { A sad necessity. Black was threatening ...Bf6 and } ( 12. d4 { fails to } Nxc3 ) 
Bxd5 13. d4 c6 { With ...Bd6 coming, Black has more than enough play for the pawn. } 
